# LB4r (k4/lb4.md §5) stress runs, EVIDENCE only. Paths under /tmp/claude-0/lb4r/ are scratch copies:
#   k4_certs_5_{n4_3,n4_4,pure}.json.gz = results/ files of the same name on branch compute/k4-frontier (PR #26) at 2809bb4 (read-only);
#   every other core list is committed as results/k4_lb4r_cores_<name>.json.gz (hard4/hard5: the cores where a weaker variant fails,
#   deep_cores: the two cores of deep5.log,
#   written by lb4_run.py --badcores, see results/k4_lb4r_weak.log; grow*: k4/lb4_randcores.py --extend; rc*: k4/lb4_randcores.py).
# -S: random profiles per core; -H: hill-climbing steps per core (restart every 500). HARD p=policy (0 need-shrinking, 1 envy-free,
# 2 none; with -P1: the number of policies that fail on their own) r=least rotations (-d1: under that policy; -d2: over all
# policies) e=rotation attempts; with -P2 the first two are swapped. Counters in -S/-H modes are per run (pol_*, rot* sum to
# runs), otherwise per run-profile pair. With a single policy (-u0, -u1, -u2) pol_needshrink counts that policy.
# The lines after 'hardest profiles found' show each profile with the state of its hardest run (for runs made before
# commit 'hill-climbing with -i1: the reported run is the hardest one', the last run evaluated; every -i1 climb was re-run).
# lb4_run.py /tmp/claude-0/lb4r/k4_certs_5_n4_3.json.gz /tmp/claude-0/lb4r/k4_certs_5_n4_4.json.gz /tmp/claude-0/lb4r/k4_certs_5_pure.json.gz -i1 -u3 -r3 -w1 -c1 -d1 -S300 --show=5
